The server pushes ptem, ldterm and ttcompat in the session's child after
setsid() instead of in pty_allocate().  On SVR4 pushing ldterm makes the
stream the controlling tty of a session leader that has none, and the
connection process is one: the child could never have the pty as its
controlling tty ("open /dev/tty failed"), so no job control, no ^C to
the foreground job, and no EOF on the master when the shell exited.
The master has no line discipline there, so the pty request's window
size and terminal modes are kept and set on the slave by the child once
the modules are pushed (before, SVR4 sessions kept ldterm's defaults:
intr DEL, erase ^h).  Elsewhere the child sets them a second time.

